Data Breach Response & Privacy Compliance Plan
1. Introduction

    Background: A security incident has occurred, potentially exposing sensitive customer personal data processed through online services.

    Purpose & Scope: This document defines the immediate containment steps, regulatory notification requirements, and long-term prevention strategies to ensure full compliance with the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).
2. Data Breach Response Strategy
    Immediate Containment & Isolation (0–24 Hours)
        Isolate compromised systems/servers to stop ongoing data exfiltration.
        Revoke compromised access credentials and patch vulnerabilities immediately.
        Preserve forensic evidence (system logs, traffic data) for analysis (GDPR Art. 32).
    Investigation & Risk Assessment (24–48 Hours)
        Determine the nature, scope, and volume of compromised personal data.
        Evaluate risks to rights and freedoms of affected individuals (e.g., identity theft, financial loss).
    Cross-Functional Escalation
        Activate the Incident Response Team (Legal, IT Security, PR, Executive Leadership, DPO).

3. Notification Strategy
Regulatory Notification (GDPR Article 33)

    Who: Relevant Lead Data Protection Authority (DPA).

    Timeline: Within 72 hours of becoming aware of the breach.

    Method: DPA online secure reporting portal.

    Content: Nature of breach, categories and approximate number of data subjects/records affected, contact details of DPO, likely consequences, and measures taken.

Data Subject Notification (GDPR Article 34)

    Who: Affected individuals (required if breach presents a high risk to rights/freedoms).

    Timeline: Without undue delay.

    Method: Direct, clear communication (email, registered letter, or targeted portal notification).

    Sample Notification Guidelines:

        Use clear, plain language avoiding technical jargon.

        Outline steps individuals can take to protect themselves (e.g., changing passwords, freezing credit).

        Provide a dedicated point of contact (DPO/Support Line) for inquiries.

4. Preventive & Corrective Measures
Technical Controls

    Encryption: Enforce end-to-end encryption for data at rest (AES-256) and in transit (TLS 1.3).

    Access Control: Implement Least Privilege Access and mandatory Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).

    Monitoring: Deploy Automated Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS/IPS) and Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) logging.

Organizational Measures

    Training: Conduct mandatory, role-specific data privacy and anti-phishing training annually.

    Policy & Audits: Update Incident Response Protocols and conduct quarterly vulnerability scans alongside annual third-party penetration testing.

5. Conclusion
A swift, organized response combined with transparent notification mitigates organizational harm and maintains customer trust. Continuous improvement, regular policy reviews, and security posture assessments will ensure long-term regulatory compliance and robust data protection.
